 "prompt": "You are one evaluator on a panel. Score the solution below to the given problem using exactly the rubric that follows (the rubric is written in Korean; score items are the English keys in parentheses). Use null for any item you cannot judge.\n\n<rubric>\n# 채점표 (문제·풀이 공통)\n\n점수 항목(1~5), \"이미 알려진 문제\", \"이해 가능 여부\" 항목에는 \"판단 불가\"를 선택할 수 있다. 확신이 없으면 추측하지 말고 판단 불가를 택한다. 풍요로움은 떠오르는 것이 없으면 비워 두며, 그것이 0점이다.\n\n## 문제 평가\n- 명확성 (clarity, 1~5): 1 = 뜻이 통하지 않거나 정의가 빠짐 · 3 = 뜻은 통하지만 모호한 부분이 있음 · 5 = 모든 기호와 조건이 정의되어 한 가지로만 읽힘\n- 난이도 (difficulty, 1~5): 1 = 학부 연습문제 수준 · 3 = 대학원생이 며칠 걸릴 수준 · 5 = 현재 알려진 방법으로는 접근이 보이지 않는 수준\n- 풍요로움 (richness_list): \"이 문제가 풀리면 쉬워지거나 자연스럽게 따라오는 다른 문제\"를 생각나는 만큼 적는다. 한 줄에 하나. 없으면 비워 둔다. 점수는 그 개수다.\n- 풀고 싶은 정도 (want_to_solve, 1~5): 1 = 전혀 · 3 = 시간이 있으면 · 5 = 지금 바로 붙잡고 싶다\n- 이미 알려진 문제 (known_problem, 예/아니오/판단 불가): 이 문제가 이미 알려진 문제·정리와 사실상 같으면 \"예\".\n\n## 풀이 평가\n- 정확성 (correctness, 1~5): 1 = 틀렸거나 핵심 단계가 없음 · 3 = 방향은 맞지만 빈틈이 있음 · 5 = 완전하고 올바름 · 유한 인스턴스가 주어진 문제는 유한 인스턴스에 대한 풀이를 기준으로 평가한다.\n- 명료성 (clarity_sol, 1~5): 1 = 따라갈 수 없음 · 3 = 노력하면 따라갈 수 있음 · 5 = 한 번 읽고 따라갈 수 있음\n- 이해 가능 여부 (understandable): 이 풀이의 핵심 아이디어를 자기 말로 다시 설명할 수 있으면 \"예\", 없으면 \"아니오\", 모르겠으면 \"판단 불가\"\n\n</rubric>\n\n<problem>\nFor $N\\in\\mathbb N$ let $r_3(N)$ denote the largest size of a set $A\\subseteq[N]=\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ containing no non-trivial $3$-term arithmetic progression, i.e. no $x,x+d,x+2d\\in A$ with $d\\neq0$. Behrend's construction gives $r_3(N)\\ge N\\exp\\big(-(c_B+o(1))\\sqrt{\\log N}\\big)$ with $c_B=2\\sqrt{2\\log 2}\\approx 2.355$, and no construction has improved this constant. Define\n$$\\kappa=\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log\\big(N/r_3(N)\\big)}{\\sqrt{\\log N}}\\in[0,c_B].$$\nProblem. Construct an infinite family of $3$-AP-free sets $A_N\\subseteq[N]$ with $|A_N|\\ge N\\exp(-c\\sqrt{\\log N})$ for some constant $c<2\\sqrt{2\\log 2}$, thereby showing $\\kappa<c_B$. More ambitiously: is $\\kappa=0$, i.e. are there $3$-AP-free sets with $|A_N|\\ge N\\exp(-(\\log N)^{\\gamma})$ for some fixed $\\gamma<1/2$ and infinitely many $N$?\n</problem>\n\n<finite_instance>\nFind a set $A\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots,20000\\}$ with $|A|\\ge1300$ containing no three distinct elements $x<y<z$ with $x+z=2y$. (For comparison: the digit construction with base-$3$ digits in $\\{0,1\\}$ gives about $520$ elements, the product of exact extremal sets for $[123]$ and $[81]$ gives $32\\cdot24=768$ elements in $[19926]$, and extrapolating the known exact values $r_3(N)$ for $N\\le123$ with the empirical local growth exponent $\\approx0.70$–$0.75$ places $r_3(20000)$ somewhere around $1100$–$1500$; $1300$ is believed to be beyond any published or easily searched construction.)\n</finite_instance>\n\n<solution>\n**Result.** A fully proved 3-AP-free set of size 704 in [1,19319]⊂[1,20000]. The target 1300 is NOT reached; status is partial.\n\n**Lemma 1 (no-carry product).** If A⊆[0,L-1] and B⊆Z_{≥0} are 3-AP-free and M≥2L-1, then C={a+Mb} is 3-AP-free with |C|=|A||B|. Proof: x_1+x_3=2x_2 gives a_1+a_3-2a_2=M(2b_2-b_1-b_3); the left side has |·|≤2(L-1)<M, so both sides vanish; 3-AP-freeness of B then A forces all indices equal.\n\n**Lemma 2.** T_6={Σ_{i=0}^{5}ε_i3^i : ε_i∈{0,1}}⊆[0,364] (64 elements) is 3-AP-free: t_1+t_3 has base-3 digits ε_i+ε'_i∈{0,1,2} with no carries, 2t_2 has digits in {0,2}, so ε_i+ε'_i=2ε''_i for all i, forcing equality.\n\n**Lemma 3.** S={0,1,5,7,8,12,18,20,21,25,26}⊆[0,26] is 3-AP-free, |S|=11=r_3(27). Exhaustive check: evens 0,8,12,18,20,26, odds 1,5,7,21,25. Midpoints of the 15 even pairs: 4,6,9,10,13,10,13,14,17,15,16,19,19,22,23; of the 10 odd pairs: 3,4,11,13,6,13,15,14,16,23. None lies in S. (S was found as X∪{12}∪(X'+18) with X={0,1,5,7,8}, X'={0,2,3,7,8}: the two outer blocks only interact via midpoints in [9,17], and m=12 works since 2m-18=6∉X+X', 12∉2X-X, -6∉2X'-X'.)\n\n**The set.** A={1+t+729s : t∈T_6, s∈S}⊆[1,19319]. By Lemma 1 with L=365, M=729=2·365-1, Lemmas 2,3, A is 3-AP-free and |A|=64·11=704. Explicitly A=∪_{s∈S}(L_0+729s), offsets 729s∈{0,729,3645,5103,5832,8748,13122,14580,15309,18225,18954}, L_0=T_6+1={1,2,4,5,10,11,13,14,28,29,31,32,37,38,40,41,82,83,85,86,91,92,94,95,109,110,112,113,118,119,121,122,244,245,247,248,253,254,256,257,271,272,274,275,280,281,283,284,325,326,328,329,334,335,337,338,352,353,355,356,361,362,364,365}.\n\nA is maximal in [1,20000]: since 2T_6-T_6=[-364,728] is a full interval, every z∈[19320,20000] equals 2y-x for some x<y∈A, so nothing can be added.\n\n**What remains.** All constructions I can certify by hand are iterated products (Lemma 1) of small extremal sets, and these are capped near r_3(N_1)r_3(N_2) with N_1N_2≈10^4, i.e. ≈700–800. Sets of size ≈1300 in [20000] (exponent ≈0.72) are only known via computer search, which I cannot run here. On the asymptotic question: no construction with c<2√(2 log 2) is known (Elkin/Green–Wolf gain only a (log N)^{1/4} factor), and the best upper bounds (Kelley–Meka, Bloom–Sisask, N exp(-c(log N)^{1/9})) are far from exp(-√(log N)); whether κ<c_B or κ=0 remains open, and I claim nothing about it.\n\nFinal answer: the 704-element 3-AP-free set listed in the candidate field; 1300 is not achieved.\n</solution>\n\n<submitted_candidate>\n[1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 11, 13, 14, 28, 29, 31, 32, 37, 38, 40, 41, 82, 83, 85, 86, 91, 92, 94, 95, 109, 110, 112, 113, 118, 119, 121, 122, 244, 245, 247, 248, 253, 254, 256, 257, 271, 272, 274, 275, 280, 281, 283, 284, 325, 326, 328, 329, 334, 335, 337, 338, 352, 353, 355, 356, 361, 362, 364, 365, 730, 731, 733, 734, 739, 740, 742, 743, 757, 758, 760, 761, 766, 767, 769, 770, 811, 812, 814, 815, 820, 821, 823, 824, 838, 839, 841, 842, 847, 848, 850, 851, 973, 974, 976, 977, 982, 983, 985, 986, 1000, 1001, 1003, 1004, 1009, 1010, 1012, 1013, 1054, 1055, 1057, 1058, 1063, 1064, 1066, 1067, 1081, 1082, 1084, 1085, 1090, 1091, 1093, 1094, 3646, 3647, 3649, 3650, 3655, 3656, 3658, 3659, 3673, 3674, 3676, 3677, 3682, 3683, 3685, 3686, 3727, 3728, 3730, 3731, 3736, 3737, 3739, 